The '''internal conflict in Potocsí''', also known as the '''Jachani-Kawsani insurgency''' or the '''eastern insurgency''', is an ongoing [[W:Armed conflict|armed conflict]] between the [[Potocsí|government of Potocsí]] and various indigenous [[W:Separatist movement|separatist movements]] in the eastern regions of the country. The conflict has its origins in longstanding grievances over [[W:Land dispossession|land dispossession]], [[W:Environmental destruction|environmental destruction]], and [[W:Political marginalization|political marginalization]] of the [[Jachani people|Jachani]] and [[Kawsani people|Kawsani]] peoples, the two largest indigenous nations in Potocsí, who together comprise approximately forty percent of the national population.

The insurgency began in earnest during the mid-1960s, evolving from localized resistance against encroaching agricultural [[W:Deforestation|deforestation]] into a coordinated armed struggle with pan-indigenous nationalist and leftist revolutionary characteristics. While the majority of insurgent factions adhere to variants of indigenous socialism—combining [[Marxist-Jianism|Marxist-Jianist]] organizational methods with traditional governance structures and cosmological frameworks—the movement has remained ideologically heterogeneous, encompassing militant autonomists, proponents of full independence, and a persistent fringe of [[W:Ethnic nationalism|ethno-nationalist]] currents advocating for the complete [[W:Ethnic cleansing|exclusion of non-indigenous populations from the eastern territories]].

The geography of the conflict has fundamentally shaped its conduct. Eastern Potocsí encompasses the densely forested slopes of the [[Vilcabamba cordillera]] and the upper watersheds of several major river systems, terrain characterized by [[W:Cloud forest|cloud forest]], steep ravines, and poor infrastructure. These conditions have rendered large portions of the region effectively ungovernable by the Potocsino state, facilitating the establishment of insurgent-controlled zones where guerrilla forces administer justice, collect taxes, and maintain parallel governance structures alongside traditional indigenous authorities. Government military operations have historically been confined to the western piedmont and major river valleys, with ''neteja'' ("cleansing") [[W:Counterinsurgency|counterinsurgency]] campaigns—characterized by selective reprisals, [[W:Forced displacement|forced displacement]], and the cultivation of [[Right-wing paramilitarism in Potocsí|paramilitary auxiliaries]]—serving as the primary mechanism for projecting state power into the contested zones.

The conflict acquired significant international dimensions during the [[Camboriú War]], when the hard-line government of [[Mário Sá Justino]] in [[Balisca]] initiated clandestine support for insurgent factions as a secondary front against the right-wing Potocsino regime. [[Baliscan Armed Forces|Baliscan military intelligence]] provided training, communication equipment, and limited arms shipments to the nascent [[Jachani Popular Liberation Front]] and affiliated groups, viewing the eastern insurgency as a means of diverting Potocsino military resources from their offensive into the [[Marañón basin]]. This support was terminated following the signing of the [[Kuluba Accords]] in 1982, which formally ended hostilities between Balisca and Potocsí and committed both governments to non-interference in each other's internal affairs.

The formal cessation of state support did not, however, eliminate Baliscan involvement in the conflict. In the post-Accords period, assistance to insurgent groups has been maintained through non-state channels, most notably the Baliscan [[Kadarist Party (Balisca)|Kadarist Party]]—which maintains organizational ties to indigenous socialist factions and has been implicated in the procurement and transshipment of military hardware—and ''[[Baliscan Mafia|La Gardunya]]'', the decentralized Baliscan criminal network whose involvement reflects the profitable opportunities presented by smuggling corridors through the eastern territories. Weapons, narcotics, and movement of personnel across the poorly demarcated [[Balisca–Potocsí border|border regions]] have allegedly been facilitated by corrupt elements within the [[Baliscan frontier administration|frontier administration]] and [[Pindorama|sympathetic communities in the Marañón basin]], where the Kadarist Party draws significant electoral support.

The contemporary phase of the conflict is defined by the intensification of [[W:Extractivism|resource extraction]] in the eastern territories and the corresponding hardening of insurgent resistance. The Potocsino government's promotion of foreign investment in [[W:Copper mining|copper mining]], timber concessions, and hydrocarbon exploration has proceeded in direct contradiction to constitutional protections for indigenous territorial rights, generating sustained mobilization among populations threatened with displacement. While government-controlled urban centers along the coast and in the western valleys have experienced significant economic growth and have largely continued to support these extractive policies, the [[Social apartheid in Potocsí|insurgent-controlled east remains mired in poverty]], with infrastructure development projects serving primarily to facilitate [[W:Resource extraction|resource extraction]] rather than human settlement. The resulting dynamic—territorial contestation over resource-rich lands, with environmental destruction as both grievance and strategy—has proven resistant to negotiated settlement, despite [[Potocsino peace process|intermittent peace processes]] and internationally mediated dialogue initiatives.

== Background ==

=== Colonial origins ===
{{Main|Volisanian colonization of Potocsí}}
The historical roots of the conflict lie in the colonial-era dispossession of indigenous lands and the establishment of [[W:Racism|racialized]] hierarchies that privileged settlers of [[Illypnian peoples|Illypnian]] descent. The [[Royal Charter of Potocsí]] (1589) had recognized indigenous communities only as wards of the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania|Crown of Volisania]], establishing legal frameworks for the alienation of communal territories through fraudulent purchase, forced indebtedness, and outright expropriation. The Jachani and Kawsani peoples, concentrated in the eastern highlands and forests, experienced less intensive settler colonial encroachment than populations in the western lowlands, preserving greater demographic and cultural integrity but also facilitating their later characterization as "uncivilized" obstacles to national development.

The [[independence of Potocsí]] from [[Volisania]] in 1821 brought formal equality before the law but little substantive change in land tenure patterns. The [[W:Classical liberalism|economically liberal]] reform movements of the mid-19th century dismantled the legal protections that had afforded indigenous communities limited security, opening indigenous territories to privatization and colonization by land companies and individual speculators. The expansion of [[W:Coffee production|coffee production]], [[W:Silver mining|silver mining]], and later [[W:Coca cultivation|industrial crops]] into the eastern piedmont accelerated displacement, generating sporadic resistance that was suppressed through military campaigns characterized by massacre and the destruction of communal infrastructure.

=== Rise of indigenous nationalism ===
The 20th century witnessed the gradual politicization of indigenous identity, accelerated by success of the [[Baliscan Revolution]], the mass mobilizations of the [[Great Adonian War]] and the consolidation of the ideological currents of [[W:Anticolonialism|anticolonialism]] and [[W:Socialism|socialism]]. The [[Jachani National Congress]] (CNJ), founded in 1953, and the [[Kawsani Confederation]] (CK), established in 1962, initially pursued strategies of cultural revitalization and electoral participation. The failure of constitutional remedies to address land seizures and the violent suppression of peaceful protests, notably the [[Massacre of Sant Isidre]] (1964), radicalized significant elements within both organizations and provided the social base for the transition to armed struggle.

== Course of the conflict ==

=== Early insurgency (1965–1978) ===
The first armed actions attributed to coordinated insurgent organizations occurred in [[1965]], with attacks on landowners and government officials in the [[province of Vilcabamba]] claimed by the nascent [[Jachani Liberation Army]] (ELJ). The movement grew rapidly in the context of the [[Crisis of 1968]], which saw the collapse of civilian government and the establishment of a military junta in [[Port Soledat]]. The junta's counterinsurgency strategy combined military sweep operations with development schemes intended to "integrate" the indigenous population through infrastructure projects and patronage networks, but these efforts largely failed to penetrate the forested highlands where insurgent bases were established.

By the mid-1970s, the insurgency had expanded beyond the Jachani heartland to incorporate Kawsani communities in the southern cordillera. The formation of the [[Kawsani Revolutionary Alliance]] (ARK) in 1976 marked the emergence of a specifically Kawsani political-military organization, distinct from but coordinating with the Jachani-dominated structures. Interfactional tensions during this period centered on strategy—the Jachani preference for [[W:People's war|protracted popular war]] versus the Kawsani advocacy for more concentrated military operations—and on the ultimate objective of the struggle, with Kawsani leaders increasingly articulating independence as a necessary goal where Jachani leadership emphasized regional autonomy.

=== Camboriú War and Baliscan intervention (1976–1982) ===
{{Main|Camboriú War}}

The [[Camboriú War]] fundamentally altered the strategic environment of the eastern insurgency. The Baliscan government of [[Mário Sá Justino]], seeking to open a second front against the Potocsino regime without committing regular forces to direct offensive operations, authorized the [[Confederal Army]] and [[Intelligence Service (Balisca)|intelligence services]] to establish contact with insurgent leadership. Training camps were established in the [[Marañón basin]], initially staffed by veterans of the [[Great Adonian War]] and later by advisors drawn from the [[Socialist Bloc]] and [[Burawai People's Army|Burawi military]]. The influx of material support, including [[W:Automatic weapons|automatic weapons]], [[W:Radio communications|radio communications]], and medical supplies, enabled insurgent forces to transition from irregular harassment to conventional military operations, including the temporary capture of several ''comarcas'' in 1980–1981.

While the Baliscan supply lines were never aimed to be sufficient to enable strategic victory, it significantly raised the costs of counterinsurgency for the Potocsino government and contributed to the decision to seek negotiated settlement with Balisca. The signing of the [[Kuluba Accords]] in August 1982 ended formal Baliscan support for the insurgency and created the framework for the normalization of diplomatic relations between the two states. The immediate consequences for the insurgency were severe: the loss of training sanctuaries forced a reversion to guerrilla tactics, and the arrest or defection of several senior commanders compromised organizational security. However, the established network of sympathizers (especially following the start of the [[Marañón oil fires]]) within Balisca, including the emerging [[Kadarist Party (Balisca)|Kadarist Party]] and elements of [[Baliscan Mafia|''La Gardunya'']], provided mechanisms for the continuation of clandestine assistance.

=== Post-Kuluba period ===
The decade following the Accords witnessed the fragmentation of the insurgent coalition under the pressure of intensified counterinsurgency operations. The [[United Front for Indigenous Dignity]] (FUDI), formed in 1985 as an attempt to coordinate Jachani, Kawsani, and smaller indigenous organizations, faced persistent challenges in maintaining unity across ideological and ethnic lines. The Potocsino government, freed from the distraction of open warfare with Balisca, was able to concentrate military resources in the east, conducting a series of major ''neteja'' ("cleansing") operations that displaced substantial populations and disrupted insurgent logistical networks. These operations drew international condemnation for severe [[W:Human rights abuses|human rights abuses]] against the local indigenous communities.

The emergence of the [[Autonomous Indigenous Brigades]] (BIA) in 1989 represented a radicalization of a portion of the movement in response to these pressures. Rejecting the leadership of the established front organizations as insufficiently militant, the BIA emphasized armed propaganda and daring attacks against symbols of state power and foreign capital, including the [[Assassination of Arturo Vidal|assassination of Minister of Development Arturo Vidal]] in 1990 and the [[destruction of the Yanacona mining facility]] in 1992. These actions drew international condemnation and reinforced the characterization of the insurgency as terrorist within the Potocsino government, resulting in harsher counterinsurgency efforts.

==== Resource wars era (1995–present) ====
The acceleration of resource extraction in the eastern territories during the late 1990s transformed the stakes and conduct of the conflict. The discovery of significant [[W:Copper deposits|copper deposits]] in the [[Vilcabamba range]], the expansion of [[W:Cocaine production|cocaine production]] in areas under insurgent control, and the promotion of [[W:Hydrocarbon exploration|hydrocarbon exploration]] through the [[Eastern Basin Initiative]] created powerful economic incentives for the militarization of indigenous territories. The insurgency responded by developing specialized units for the protection of coca cultivation and the taxation of extractive operations, generating revenues that partially offset the loss of external support.

The contemporary phase has been characterized by the entrenchment of territorial division: government-controlled western valleys and coastal cities, contested corridor zones of intensive counterinsurgency and guerrilla presence, and insurgent-administered forest and highland territories where the state projects power only through sporadic airstrikes and special forces raids. Negotiations during this period have repeatedly stalled over the question of indigenous territorial autonomy, with government proposals for "special development zones" rejected by insurgent leadership as inadequate to guarantee cultural and environmental protection.

== Support networks ==

=== Baliscan connections ===
Despite the formal commitments of the Kuluba Accords, Baliscan organizations have maintained significant connections to the insurgency. The [[Kadarist Party (Balisca)|Kadarist Party]], which emerged from a split in the [[Revolutionary Front]] precisely over the question of support for continued armed struggle, maintains organizational ties to the [[Jachani Popular Liberation Front]] and has been implicated in the procurement of military equipment through front companies and sympathetic officials. Party cadres in the [[Marañón basin]], where the Kadarists draw significant electoral support from indigenous communities, have allegedly facilitated the movement of personnel and materials across the border.

[[Baliscan Mafia|''La Gardunya'']], the decentralized criminal network known internationally as the Baliscan Mafia, operates in the conflict zone primarily through economic rather than ideological motivations. The ungoverned spaces of eastern Potocsí provide valuable transit corridors for narcotics smuggling routes connecting [[Abyala|Abyalan]] production zones to coastal shipping points, and insurgent forces have reportedly established cooperative arrangements with trafficking organizations in exchange for weapons, intelligence, and financial support. The distinction between politically motivated insurgency and economically driven criminality has become increasingly blurred in these arrangements.

=== Other ===
International involvement in the conflict remains circumscribed but persistent. Beyond residual [[Baliscan people|Baliscan]] connections, the insurgency has reportedly received diplomatic support from [[Burawa]] and other [[Socialist Bloc]] states, which have provided platforms for insurgent representatives at international forums and may facilitate indirect material assistance. The Potocsino government has cultivated security partnerships with right-leaning states in [[Illypnia]] and [[Kaftia]], receiving counterinsurgency training and equipment that has been deployed in the eastern campaigns. The strategic significance of the conflict has been amplified by the region's position along trans-[[Abyala|Abyalan]] narcotics trafficking routes, with the ungoverned spaces of eastern Potocsí serving as transit corridors for cocaine and precursor chemicals moving between production zones in the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] highlands and coastal shipping points (in [[Balisca]], [[Asikyira]] and [[Lachase]])—a dynamic that has encouraged the involvement of [[W:Transnational organized crime|transnational criminal networks]] and complicated efforts to distinguish politically motivated insurgency from economically predatory violence.

== Analysis, impact and casualties ==
The insurgent coalition, while unified in opposition to the Potocsino state and commitment to [[W:Indigenous sovereignty|indigenous sovereignty]] and [[W:Self-determination|self-determination]], encompasses significant internal tensions that have periodically erupted into interfactional violence. The Jachani majority within the movement has generally favored a strategy of regional autonomy within a restructured federal Potocsí, emphasizing [[W:Land reform|land reform]] and constitutional recognition of indigenous sovereignty. Kawsani organizations have tended toward more radical positions, with significant constituencies advocating full independence as the only viable guarantee of cultural survival. The fringe of indigenous supremacist currents—[[Runisme|propounding doctrines of ethnic purity]] and the expulsion of [[W:Mestizo|Mestís]] and [[W:Creole people|creole]] populations from indigenous territories—complicates the political positioning of the movement internationally and creates operational friction with more moderate factions. The country's [[Mestís Potocsino|Mestís population]], historically subject to discrimination by the white settler-descended elite that continues to dominates coastal politics, occupies an ambiguous position within the conflict, vulnerable to violence from supremacist insurgent elements while simultaneously excluded from full participation in the state structures that claim to represent their interests.

Estimates of casualties and displacement remain contested, with government figures typically undercounting insurgent-associated deaths while human rights organizations have documented extensive extrajudicial killings and mass displacement resulting from counterinsurgency operations. The conflict has generated approximately 800,000 [[W:Internally displaced person|internally displaced persons]], concentrated in [[W:Urban area|urban peripheries]] where indigenous migrants encounter discrimination and economic marginalization. The durability of the insurgency—now spanning six decades with no plausible military resolution—reflects the fundamental incompatibility between the Potocsino state's development model and the territorial and cultural claims of the indigenous populations of the eastern territories, a contradiction that successive negotiations have failed to bridge.

